+# Generation prompts: styles 273–276
+
+Tool: built-in image_gen. All images freshly generated from text, without reference images. Final PNGs resized to 1024×1024 using sips.
+
+## 273
+
+Create one fresh, square 1024x1024 illustration entirely from text, without any reference image.
+Use case: illustration-story.
+Style is the absolute priority: Mike Mignola Hellboy-style comic art, a single powerful gothic comic panel. Massive interlocking pools of solid black shadow, blocky angular shapes and anatomy, broad flat silhouettes, extremely economical line detail, muted stone gray and desaturated olive with mustard ochre as the main warm accent. No gradients, photographic modeling, realistic lens effects or painterly rendering.
+Subject: a woman in her early 30s with curly auburn shoulder-length hair, freckles, round tortoiseshell glasses and a mustard-yellow knit sweater, quietly enjoying coffee; a croissant nearby.
+Composition and scene: a wide, monumental view from below inside roofless gothic ruins. The seated woman is a compact angular full-body figure on a fractured stone step in the lower part of the panel, lifting a small coffee cup in one hand. The croissant rests on a stone ledge within reach. Vast black pointed arches, fractured pillars, heavy shadow and a few graphic shapes of sky dominate the composition. Hair, face, hands, sweater and stone are all reduced to the distinctive bold angular graphic language, never an ordinary portrait with a filter. Freckles and glasses remain legible with just a few marks. Her modest coffee break feels calm amid the imposing ruins.
+No text, lettering, captions, speech balloons, signatures, logos or watermarks. One single panel filling the square.
+
+## 274
+
+Create one fresh, square 1024x1024 comic page entirely from text, without using any reference image or prior generated image.
+Use case: illustration-story.
+Style priority: Chris Ware graphic novel page. Meticulously precise clean geometric linework, almost architectural construction, flat muted pastel colors, tiny simplified people, rigidly organized but inventive diagrammatic panel subdivisions and generous cream gutters. Authentically designed sequential comic art, not a photograph with a filter, not a conventional large portrait, no photorealism, no gradients.
+Show the quiet morning coffee routine of a woman in her early 30s with curly auburn shoulder-length hair, freckles, round tortoiseshell glasses and a mustard-yellow knit sweater; a croissant accompanies her coffee. Translate her completely into the style's simplified anatomy: round geometric head, very small features, blocky torso, minimal curved limbs, schematic curly hair and circular spectacles. In the few closer panels retain several tiny freckles.
+Composition: an intricate complete wordless comic page with about twenty small panels of different sizes in a carefully aligned grid. A wider architectural cutaway of her small apartment anchors the upper part. Smaller sequential panels follow her entering the kitchen, reaching for the coffee pot, pouring coffee, placing a croissant on a plate, carrying them to the table and finally sipping quietly beside a window. Include precise close details of the cup, hands, croissant, window and chair to pace the silence, with tiny full-body profile views of her moving through the apartment. Consistent room geography and character across the panels. Time passing is expressed entirely through small changes in gesture and objects, not symbols or writing.
+Palette: cream paper, faded pale blue, muted dusty rose, soft sage, dull auburn and small mustard accents. Color is perfectly flat with delicate crisp dark outlines. Emotional mood: intimate, quiet, contemplative domestic routine.
+No text of any kind anywhere: no title, letters, numerals, captions, labels, speech bubbles, signatures or watermarks. A single square artwork containing the complete multi-panel page.
+
+## 275
+
+Create one fresh, square 1024x1024 illustration entirely from text. No reference images and no use of earlier generated images.
+Use case: illustration-story.
+Style is the absolute priority: Charles Burns Black Hole-style comic art. Heavy glossy-looking black brush inks on white paper, pristine and eerie smoothly swollen contours, rigorous high-contrast black-and-white graphic design, smooth sculptural ink shapes, unsettling suburban 1970s atmosphere. Everything is drawn with brush and pen, with no photographic texture, no grayscale wash, no color and no gradients.
+Subject: a woman in her early 30s with curly auburn shoulder-length hair, freckles, round tortoiseshell glasses and a mustard-yellow knit sweater, enjoying a cup of coffee; a croissant nearby. Interpret the auburn hair, tortoiseshell glasses and mustard knit entirely through black-and-white ink patterns, not actual color. Adult proportions rendered in the uncanny, slightly stiff and smoothly stylized anatomy of the comic.
+Composition: an extremely intimate close-up from table height, her face in three-quarter profile occupying much of the square, with the dark oval of a coffee cup just below her lips, one carefully inked hand holding it. Her dense shoulder-length curls form shiny flowing black masses with a few slender white highlights; dotted freckles, round patterned spectacles and dark eyes remain clear. The croissant on its plate occupies the near foreground at the bottom. A narrow background glimpse shows a 1970s suburban breakfast nook, patterned wallpaper, a window, a silent low ranch house and telephone wires outside. Window reflections and oddly elongated shadows produce subtle unease while she appears quietly absorbed in her coffee. A strongly asymmetric crop and strangely still atmosphere.
+Pure stark black ink and white paper only. No monsters needed. No text, captions, lettering, speech balloons, numerals, signatures, watermarks or logos. A single panel filling the square.
+
+## 276
+
+Create one fresh, square 1024x1024 illustration entirely from text. No reference images and do not reuse any earlier generated image.
+Use case: illustration-story.
+Style is the absolute priority: Robert Crumb-style underground comix. Obsessively dense hand-drawn pen crosshatching, restless scratchy ink lines, thick expressive cartoon outlines, exaggerated rubbery anatomy, large heavy legs, huge clunky lace-up shoes, homely funny caricature faces and dynamic urban social observation. Black ink on slightly off-white newsprint, with uneven printing texture. No color, no photorealism, no photographic lighting, no digital smooth shading.
+Subject: a woman in her early 30s with curly auburn shoulder-length hair, freckles, round tortoiseshell glasses and a mustard-yellow knit sweater, enjoying a coffee with a croissant nearby. Render the auburn hair, tortoiseshell glasses and mustard sweater entirely through monochrome linework and hatching. Her sweater has dense woven ribbing, her hair is a riot of springy ink curls, her glasses are comically round, and her freckled face has a relaxed lively grin.
+Composition: a wide energetic full-body street vignette with exaggerated low-eye-level perspective. She strides across the foreground along a busy city sidewalk, her enormously clunky shoes prominent, elbows loose, one hand bringing a takeaway coffee cup to her mouth, the other holding an open bakery paper bag with a visible croissant protruding near the coffee. She is a robust, gangly rubber-limbed caricature with intentionally distorted proportions, not a realistic attractive portrait. The lively street around her contains a few amusing pedestrians, a curb, parked older cars, stoops, brick walls, a cafe doorway and densely hatched pavement. Tilted rhythms, lively gestures and busy observed detail make the entire scene feel like an authentic underground comix panel. Blank awnings and unmarked shop windows.
+All shading must be made of crosshatched pen strokes and black ink patches. No text of any kind, captions, speech bubbles, signage, numbers, signatures or watermarks. Single-panel artwork filling the square.
+

